It seems that the problem is not the definition of censorship, but
the correct place for relcom in the analogy with the press (mass media).

If one takes a newspaper, then an author is subject to the whim of the
editor but any control by the post office and/or other state organisation
will be a censorship. Dima Volodin reflects the point of view of an editor,
and therefore any accusation of censorship is senseless. The opponents assume
relcom news is equivalent to common media space and therefore speak of
censorship. I am not in a position to decide what is more applicable to the
case, but current state of discussion is that opponents speak of different
(implicitly) things and are not going to understand each other.

Another misunderstandnis occurs from the analogy drawn to the Usenet. As far
as I understand, Usenet is neither a private business, nor a subbusiness of
Internet. Therefore, no one can actually claim any control over it even if
one likes to do it. Relcom news, as put by Dima, is a service of relcom, and
is therefore subject to all its regulations. The only linkage is that the
relcom.*.* groups can be found in the Usenet groups list (although I never
managed to get anything) and that they use the same software. I can easily
imagine (knowing ex-SU habits) that relcom advertises these groups
__as Usenet groups__. If so, all criticism is right. To correct
the situation, relcom should clearly and explicitly distinguish relcom.*.*
groups from Usenet. After this, Usenet should re-evaluate, whether it should
allow the relcom.*.* groups to appear in its lists (not necessarily negative,
maybe just attach "moderated" clause).

Next problem is the ex-soviet level of law education and abiding, but this is
a 'force majeur'  to the question under discussion.


