	I see the point of the author, but I'm hard-pressed to come up with
a decent solution.  If Harvard continues to accept the alt.sex.* hierarchy,
then, yes it could be said that student tuition is being used to pipe in
stories that some might consider offensive.  Sure, I know the "if you don't
like it, don't read it" argument, but the fact remains that the Harvard
student is still paying for it.

	If Harvard opts to censor the group, the outcry of "Censorship!"
would be heard far and wide, and it would generate negative publicity for
the university.  Access couldn't be "restricted" to those who specifically
request the newsgroups, because 1) it would infringe on the privacy of the
reader, and 2) anyone who knows squat about usenet knows that the cost of
receiving Usenet is fixed, regardless of whether or not newsgroups are
censored (blocked) or restricted.

	While I personally find some of the stories on alt.sex.* to be
morally reprehensible, I am not (and have no desire to be) in any position
to decide what should and shouldn't be allowed.  If enough organizations
decide to censor alt.sex.* groups, it can and probably will escalate into 
a serious avalanche of censorship laws that will make more topics taboo
on Usenet.  I think that a modicum of tolerance is a better answer than
censorship.  A serious Harvard student should have more important things to 
think about than offensive stories on Usenet.

