FOOTHILL-DE ANZA COMMUNITY COLLEGE DISTRICT, CALIFORNIA, USA

INTRODUCTION - FIRST AMENDMENT FIRST!

Foothill College, Silicon Valley, California, is a reputed junior college
located on a beautiful site, a few miles south from Stanford University
down Highway 280. In an effort to preserve its fine reputation and
maintain the status quo, Foothill's administration decided a few years
ago to suppress all criticism by silencing its faculty and students. It
enacted one of the most restrictive speech codes among state colleges,
instituted a prior restraint system requiring advance approval of public
expression, and simultaneously repealed its 'due process' procedures to
expedite dismissal or exile of offending students.

A grass-roots coalition known as FIRST AMENDMENT FIRST! has been
constituted by a nucleus of Foothill students. Its aim is to break down
the speech code in vigor at Foothill by way of class action litigation.
It has already filed two lawsuits in the federal courts. The first one
makes use of the recent Leonard law to challenge the ban on free
expression and the censorship system, while the second seeks to have the
speech code stricken down as unconstitutional.

Foothill's restrictive prior restraint system, which censors all student
expression the nomenclatura deems inappropriate, recklessly defies the
intent that the California Legislature expressed when in 1991 it declared
that "every person has the right to speak out, to poke fun, and to stir
up controversy without fear of criminal prosecution" and when in 1992 it
confirmed that "a student has the same right to free speech on campus as
he or she enjoys when off campus."

While the criminal element of society has everything to fear from
uncensored free speech, why would public scrutiny alarm Foothill's top
officials if their actions are as virtuous as they say (*)? At a time
where Foothill College sinks deeper by the week into the Dark Ages of
Stalinist repression and gulag, FIRST AMENDMENT FIRST! keeps working to
bring about a new era of glasnost. In view of the great importance of
this universal right, let us always be prepared to defend our right to
freedom of speech (*). Remember, our ancestors died fighting for it.

