NEW LAW GRANTS STUDENTS FIRST AMENDMENT PROTECTION

STANFORD'S GREY INTERPRETATION OF SPEECH MAY VIOLATE THE CONSTITUTION

ACCORDING TO LEONARD LAW

A new California law, enacted January 1, 1993, grants Stanford students
the same freedom of speech guaranteed by the First Amendment to all
Americans off campus.

The California law protects the speech of students who attend
non-religiously affiliated public and private high schools, colleges, and
universities. It prohibits these institutions from making or enforcing
rules which would discipline students for speech or other communications
violations which are normally considered legal under the First Amendment
to the United States Constitution or Article One, Section Two of the
California Constitution.

The Leonard law, the new statute of the California Education Code, gives
students the necessary tools to fight speech codes that may be
unconstitutional. Students are now allowed to "commence a civil action to
obtain appropriate injunctive and declaratory relief," and may be awarded
attorney's fees in a successful civil action suit. Students may sue just
because a speech code exists, according to the Leonard law, regardless of
whether the speech code is enforced by the university or not.        

