cosell@world.std.com (Bernie Cosell) writes:

> Did it ever occur to any of you guys to just go and get the
> newsgroups you want fed *directly*?  That is, does the ban actually
> *ban*the*material* or just not allow it through the university
> system?  One way to approach this is instead of proposing solutions
> which will waste a lot of everyones time and money, you could just
> render their decision moot: there are a zillion places you can get
> access to the groups you're so hot about, so just go and access
> them on your own.   Even better, all you need is *one* enterprising
> student to arrange a feed of the affected groups to a PC in his
> dorm room and you'd be back on the air...  It'd be interesting to
> see what happens [and a far more substantial matter to be worrying
> about] when/if the material *appears*on*campus*anyway*.

Yes, I appreciate that there is no problem with getting the
information. In fact, that's what I think is cool about the Internet. A
ban is no longer absolute - in fact, it's a bit of a laugh. So, I felt
that there were two approaches to take:

1) say F**k it (I'm censoring this phrase lest small children or
university students see it) and go out and get access for myself while
simultaneously letting the issue go and letting the offending
administrators, system admins off the hook or;

2) press the issue, open it up to discussion on our campus, have
everybody discuss privacy, electronic freedom, whether or not the admin
should be in the business of telling the university what not to read.
Then, and only then we can then try to get the Senate to approve a more
enlightened policy that holds freedom of expression in its rightful
place and which insists on the right of students to participate in this
global network with the full support and assistance of the University.
After all, this is supposed to be an institution of learning dedicated
to exploration and free communication. So, it's time to make it one.

I appreciate that the second approach is by far the most difficult, but
having seen what people at other places are doing, I felt that this was
the least I could do. Besides, I don't even really read any alt.*
groups! But, I know of many others who used to or would like to and who
now can not. It is a larger issue than just my own access.

Also, I just recieved clarification from the VP Academic and Provost of
UVic on whether or not there is a ban in place. He stated that there is
no ban, but that the groups in question were available to any student
who could prove their "academic merit". For some reason, it is not
necessary to prove academic merit in order to look at the rec.* groups,
or to take out library books dealing with many of the issues embodied
in various alt.* groups.

The American Library Association definition of censorship (is there a
definition from Canada???) is as follows (according to the statement
stored in ftp.eff.org in pub/academic/library:
---
Censorship -- The change in the access status of material, made by a
governing authority or its representatives. Such changes include:
exclusion, restriction, removal, or age/grade level changes.
---
This is exactly what we are experiencing in UVic. Access to certain
portions of the Internet has been restricted. The effort required for
the average user of computer systems and news readers at this
University is such that they will for the most part be unable to
participate in the entire alt.* hierarchy. As I said before, it is a
"ban by making things difficult" not an "outright ban".

Anyways, sorry this was so long ... I think there's lots of work to do
here.

P.S. The gist of the replies seem to say to me, "Screw them, just go
get what you want, and let them play their politically correct,
head-in-the-sand games", but I am afraid that if we always take that
stance, then we will soon be faced with laws that we never challenged.
Look around the other articles in this newsgroup for example... Am I
being silly and idealistic or is it right to stand up and call bullshit
when people make silly political decisions in order to cover their
asses??  Comments?????
-- 
