If anyone has files like that on a computer disk which isn't either
(a) physically removed from the machine when not in use and/or (b)
encrypted with some sufficiently secure method deserves what they get.

Trying to tie this back to the example of a physical office (which
isn't always a good one), keeping a file non-encrypted is like leaving
a file in an unlocked office cabinet; if someone has to look through
your office for a legitimate purpose, the chance of them accidentally
finding the document is too great.  For example, if someone I work
with needs a book, paper, or some other document out of my office,
they know that I don't mind them getting it themself if I am not
around.  However, I keep my desk locked when I am away, and make sure
to keep personal documents in the desk.  Similarly, files on our
system which for whatever reason would be damaging if released are
encrypted on the disk.  It's not that I am worried about someone
looking through my files as part of a security audit (since I am
basically the person who handles security on our machines and make a
strong effort not to "spy" on other's files except when asked to by
the owner of a file), but I am worried about the users who may be
looking for a file that they know I should have a copy of or the
(potential) cracker who breaks into our systems.

