In article <2c95r5$f73@eff.org>, Carl M. Kadie <kadie@eff.org> wrote:
>morgan@engr.uky.edu (Wes Morgan) writes:
>>If the SPA (or whoever) comes in and find bootleg DOS software sitting 
>>in a user's diskspace on my server, who's going to pay the fine (or go 
>>to court)?  If recent SPA events are any indication, the user won't be
>>dealing with it at all (according to the media); the service provider
>>will be dealing with all that rot.
>
>What recent events? The U. of Oregon case apparently involved
>copyrighted software on *public* disk space. 

I was referring to the incidents discussed in _Information Week_
some months ago.  My use of the word "recent" may have been in-
appropriate.  8)

>The settlement did *not*
>require the university to search private disk space.

I'm in a bind on this one.  Users may have diskspace on either their 
private PC or the public server; in some cases, operations dictate that 
several people/offices share common diskspace.  If three offices are 
sharing a disk directory on the public server, who is responsible?  You 
can't very well say that all XX people with write access are responsible, 
so the responsibility may fall to the service provider.

Many PC LAN systems (LAN Manager and PC-NFS, to name two) also allow
DOS users to access disk resources on Unix systems.  I, for instance,
have a nice big "dosstuff" directory on one our of Unix boxes.  Given
the Unix file system permission facility, it's easy to allow (inten-
tionally or inadvertently) system-wide write access to one's private
directories.  Now we get to think about things like the use of a Unix
host to distribute bootlegs of PC software.  When a machine has XXXX
users and allows open doors among them, how can we assign any measure
of responsibility to the individual user?  

What is the SPA's definition of "public" and "private" diskspace?
If the user doesn't own (or have primary responsibility for) the
machine, is their diskspace really *theirs*?

It's one thing to talk about "invading privacy" on a private, single-
user PC; it's another thing to talk about small-group shared resources,
and it's yet another when we're talking about resources shared by 
thousands of users.  How does the notion of "private space" scale
in the eyes of the "authorities?"

--Wes

ps> Compare and contrast this question with the "Should my anonymous
    FTP server allow uploads?" discussions.

