I respect Mr. Kadie and what he's trying to accomplish here on the net
in defending principles of academic freedom and fighting against true
cases of censorship.  However, in posting this article that merely has
"CENSORSHIP" in its Subject, several important details are being left out.

Mr. Herman chose to take a thread about the Lambda Amateur Radio Club
(an organization of Gay and Lesbian radio hobbyists) and their efforts
to get a classified ad into a mainstream radio publication (QST) and
turn it into an extended flame about animal sex, bathroom cruising,
and other potty stories.  Without commenting on the merits of the
organization and its case against the American Radio Relay League
(I'm straight, BTW), I can say with certainty that Mr. Herman took the
thread way out of the scope of the newsgroups in question (which are
devoted to hobbyist radio, and not "potty stories"), discredited
himself, and his employer (The University of Hawaii).

Mr. Herman was asked by several people to use some restraint.  In no
case was he told not to post at all, nor was he threatened with
censorship just for having a disagreeable viewpoint.  He was instead
told to take it to a more appropriate forum, such as alt.sex or 
alt.politics.homosexuality.  (In other words, obey reasonable
restrictions on "time, place, and manner" of speech, which courts have
ruled to be permissible under the First Amendment).  You can still read
much of Mr. Herman's rantings, as many of his posts have Expires headers
with dates well into the future (some as distant as April 1, 1995).

Mr. Herman could be viewed as disrupting a group of people who wished to
freely associate with a common goal (such as an organization).  This
group did not wish to be continually confronted by Mr. Herman's
bizarre arguments linking amateur radio with certain bodily functions.
We asked him to not violate our freedom of expression, and notified his
system administrators of the situation.  If they chose to take away
his account arbitrarily, then that, and only that, would truly be
"censorship."  If Mr. Herman chose to barge into a local meeting of a
radio club and start recruiting members for his "Bestiality Amateur
Radio Fraternity," for example, he would probably have been shown the
door (possibly with assistance from the local law enforcement
authority).

There is no absolute right of free speech on Usenet.  For one thing,
it's not a common carrier.  For another, there are probably legal and
appropriate restrictions on "time, place, and manner" that need to be
obeyed to avoid infringing on other's rights.  Mr. Herman showed no
consideration for other's rights, then whines about "censorship" when he
can't have his way.  This is hardly a case of his rights being violated.

--
