Public universities have very limited authority to restrict the speech
of their students, perhaps because they have so often abused the
authority they are given.

pschleck@cwis.unomaha.edu (Paul W Schleck KD3FU) writes:

[...]
>He was instead told to take it to a more appropriate forum, such as
>alt.sex or alt.politics.homosexuality.  (In other words, obey
>reasonable restrictions on "time, place, and manner" of speech, which
>courts have ruled to be permissible under the First Amendment).
[...]

The legal sense of "time, place, and manner" requires that
restrictions be content neutral.

   "Similarly, we have upheld reasonable -time, place, or manner-
   restrictions, but only if they are -justified without reference to
   the content of the regulated speech.- Ward v.  Rock Against Racism,
   491 U.  S. 781, 791 (1989) (internal quotation marks omitted); see
   also Clark v. Community for Creative Non-Violence, 468 U. S. 288,
   298 (1984) (noting that the O'Brien test differs little from the
   standard applied to time, place, or manner restrictions).
   [RAV v. St. Paul]

[...]
>Mr. Herman could be viewed as disrupting a group of people who wished to
>freely associate with a common goal (such as an organization).

"Offensive" is not the same as "disruptive". I do not believe that his
articles, easily ignored or killed, were disruptive enough to give the
U. of Hawaii justification in restricting them. To be disruptive
enough to justify state action, the articles would have had to
materially and substantially interfered with the forum. [Tinker v. Des
Moines. See the referenced material on _Cohen_ for an explanation of
why there is no right not to be offended.]

As for your right to associate. if the forum had been moderated and
Mr. Herman had forged approvals this would be a good argument. It is
not a good argument when applied to a open forum.

Let me suggest what I think could be your best argument. The Supreme
Court's Public Forum Doctrine allows the government (e.g. U. of
Hawaii) to restrict the topic in government-created forums. This
sounds like just what you want.

There are, however, limitations. The Public Forum Doctrine does not
allowed the government to discriminate on the basis of viewpoint. It
also doen't allow the government to act just because some expression
causes a "hue and cry" of public objection.

If you could show that the U. of Hawaii has a policy restricting
unmoderated newsgroups to certain topics and that this policy is
consistently enforced without regard to viewpoint, tone, or
offensiveness, then the Public Forum Doctrine would give U. of Hawaii
authority to act against Mr. Herman.

- Carl

