In article <CGLy3A.MI5@cs.uiuc.edu>, Carl M Kadie <kadie@cs.uiuc.edu> wrote:
>morgan@engr.uky.edu (Wes Morgan) writes:
>
>>Let's suppose that a given piece of illegal/prohibited software has a
>>unique identifier. [...] Let's futher suppose that I write a script
>>which, without displaying ANY other information, merely checks every
>>text file on the system for these "signature" strings.  (This is the
>>approach taken by DOS virus scanners.)  Those files which contain the
>>signature strings are printed in a report for the admin.
>>
>>Have I violated user privacy?
>
>It may depend. Speaking hypothetically:
>  Did you, as a sys admin, get "higher authorization" to start these searches?

I would expect that authorization from higher office(s) would be necessary.
I sure wouldn't do something like this without the full knowledge and per-
mission of my supervisor.

>  Did you tell your users about the searches?

Of course; how can you prosecute a user for possessing a program that
they did not know was prohibited?  I envision something like a "pro-
hibited software list," which would be updated "on the fly" and kept
online.  This file would also include a provision for exceptions, based
on academic/research need.

>  Do you allow your users access to your search script so that
>       1) they can see exactly what you are actually searching for

I'll take issue with this idea.  What good is a search if everyone
knows the target.  If I say "I'm looking for the ASCII string "XXYYZ(",
the not-so-nice users will simply remove/change that string.  The actual
search strings must be confidential if we're trying to be effective.

The users will (should) know what programs/code I'm searching for, as
stated above; why wouldn't that be sufficient?

>       2) they can search themselves

I don't see anything wrong with letting them run the search program on
their own directories.

>  What is the procedure for following up on hits?

That would depend on each site.  Personally, I'd simply ask the user
(in person) about it.  99% of the time, it's just curiosity or the
"lure of the forbidden;" in those cases, I merely take the opportunity
to remind the user of relevant University policies/procedures/discipline
and send them on their way.  I don't put them on some "watch list," and
I don't take any special action; in fact, I'll often create bogus data
for them to analyze! If they're only interested in the techniques, they
don't need "live" data, right?  I have created a proper, but completely
bogus, /etc/passwd for this very purpose...

The other 1% are those who are actually trying to "do something."  Those
folks get a little chat, the same reminder of University policy, and notice
that I can let one incident slide; if they do it again, in full knowledge
of the policies of the University, they're going to face charges.  I make
the same offer of "proper, but bogus" data on which they can experiment.

I've never had a repeat offender; some folks have requested the bogus
working data, and I've obliged them.  To me, this approach builds their
knowledge, reduces my worry and increases the trust between us.  It hasn't
failed yet.

--Wes

