From caf-talk Caf Nov 17 22:13:02 1993
From: morgan@engr.uky.edu (Wes Morgan)
Newsgroups: comp.org.eff.talk,comp.admin.policy,misc.legal,alt.privacy,alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk
Subject: Searching for Prohibited Software: A Respectful Approach
Date: 18 Nov 1993 03:08:27 GMT
Message-ID: <2ceovb$r39@s.ms.uky.edu>

OK, the first round of discussion (under the misnomer of the CERT Advisory
issue) seems complete; I'd like to restate the issue, and my approach to
an equitable solution, in a clear fashion.  As always, comments are en-
couraged...


SOFTWARE PROHIBITIONS AND USER PRIVACY: A COMPROMISE

 For whatever reason, some sites prohibit (or would like to prohibit) the 
 possession/execution of particular software packages.  In most cases, the 
 prohibition has its roots in system security concerns.  For the purposes
 of this discussion, let us assume that some sort of prohibition has been
 placed in effect.  Our task, then, is to develop a means by which the sys-
 tem administrator can enforce the prohibition without undue violation of
 user privacy.

1) USERS MUST BE INFORMED OF THE PROHIBITION(S) IN ADVANCE

 Once the decision is made to prohibit a particular piece of software, the
 users should be informed of the prohibition.  I suggest that a "Prohibited
 Software List" be maintained online.  This list should include the names
 of the package(s), the effective date of the prohibition, *and* the ratio-
 nale behind the prohibition.  For example, our hypothetical list might in-
 clude an entry such as this:

   cloak.c - Allows users to "vanish" from the system.  Execution of this 
             program violates Section XXX of the User Policy.    11/25/93

 It is important to include a "grace period" before enactment of the pro-
 hibition.  A grace period will protect users who may currently own copies
 of the software; it will also give them a chance to remove it from the sys-
 tem themselves.  If the software in question is discovered during the grace
 period, the user should receive nothing more than a warning.

2) BE PREPARED TO MAKE EXCEPTIONS

 In an academic environment, computer systems are often used for individual
 education/research, outside the realm of faculty/staff supervision.  Users
 engaged in such individualized activities should be given the chance to jus-
 tify their possession of a prohibited software package.  Ideally, this pro-
 cess would take place during the aforementioned "grace period."  Even if
 possession of the software is discovered during the enforcement stage, the
 user should be given an opportunity to justify their use/possession of the
 software.

 In some cases, the software can be used in a "controlled" environment.
 Just as some vaccines use "killed" viruses, we can used "killed" input
 data for this purpose.  For instance, it is trivial to create a properly
 formatted, but worthless, password file for use with password cracking
 programs; worthless collections of Ethernet traffic may be created for
 use with network traffic analysis programs.  The use of such "controlled" 
 input data can often provide a compromise between the curious user and 
 the security-conscious administrator.

3) SEARCHING FOR VIOLATIONS OF THE PROHIBITION

 Obviously, any examination of a user's files is a violation of their
 privacy; however, the decision to prohibit the possession/execution
 of particular software packages has made such examinations necessary.

 To minimize the intrusion, it is essential that the examination be
 unattended; the administrator should not be examining the content of
 individual files visually.  To that end, I suggest the use of "signa-
 ture strings."  Those of you familiar with PC virus scanners should
 already be familiar with the concept; however, we'll start this pro-
 cess at the beginnning.

	- The administrator selects a signature string from the pro-
	  hibited software package.  This signature string must be as
	  unique as possible; in fact, several strings should be selec-
	  ted from each package, in order to minimize the possibility
	  of a "false positive" result.  (Note: it is possible to search
	  both text files (source code) and binary files (executables)
	  with this method; the Unix strings(1) command can often pro-
	  vide signature strings for compiled versions of prohibited
	  software.

	  [ NOTE: Those sites which desire to make a distinction between  ]
 	  [ the possession of software and its actual execution can often ]
	  [ make that distinction with a careful selection of search cri- ]
	  [ teria.  For instance, one might search for signature strings  ]
	  [ found only in binary (compiled) versions of the software. This]
	  [ may not always be possible, but it can be helpful.            ]

	- The users should not know the specific signature strings; such 
	  foreknowledge would allow violators to modify their code to avoid 
	  detection, and those who can justify their possession of the soft-
	  ware have no need to avoid detection.  Since the users have al-
	  ready been informed of the prohibition, via the aforementioned 
	  "Prohibited Software List," I do not believe that the signature 
	  strings used for detection should be released to the public.

	- The search program should do nothing more than generate a
	  list of files which contain the signature strings.  The con-
	  tent of the user files should *not* be examined or modified 
	  in any way at this time; however, it may be wise to make a 
	  tape backup of suspected files for future reference.  (Of course, 
	  this tape backup should be destroyed if no disciplinary action 
	  results from this procedure.)

	- The search program should be available for execution by all
	  users; while this may allow violators to test their conceal-
	  ment of the software, it will reassure those users who have
	  no desire to violate site policy.  This will also give the
	  users another opportunity to eliminate "false positive" re-
	  sults or justify their possession of prohibited software.
	
	- The search program should be executed at random; a regular
	  day/time of execution would allow violators additional op-
	  portunity to camouflage their activities.

	- The results of the search should NOT be distributed outside
	  the responsible operations staff.  As a "check and balance"
	  mechanism, the results should be reviewed with the appropriate
	  higher authority before any further action is taken.  Under no 
	  circumstances should the results be made public.

4) DEALING WITH THE RESULTS

 If the search program indicates that a violation of the prohibition
 may have occured, the administrator should contact the user at the
 earliest opportunity.  When meeting with the user, the administrator
 should explain the prohibition and search procedures and inform the
 user that one (or more) of their files are suspect.  At this point, the
 user should be given an opportunity to justify their possession of the
 prohibited software package.  With (and only with) the user's explicit 
 permission, the administrator may visually inspect the suspect files at 
 this time.  If the files in question are not in violation of any prohi-
 bitions, the issue is closed; the search results, and any tape backups 
 made, should be destroyed.  If the user does not grant permission for 
 such action, the matter should be remanded to the appropriate authority; 
 the user should be informed of this, and told to expect further communi-
 cation regarding this incident from the relevant authority.  To avoid 
 "tampering with the evidence," a backup tape of the user's filespace 
 may be made at this time; the user should be informed of this backup 
 tape and the rationale behind its creation.


5) THE ADMINISTRATOR'S WORK IS DONE

 At this point, the resolution of this matter lies solely with the
 appropriate judicial/disciplinary authority.  While the administra-
 tor may be directed to produce evidence, such as the aforementioned
 search results and/or tape backups, he should have no place in the
 actual due process decision.  In the interest of user privacy and
 confidentiality, the results of the search and subsequent events
 should not be discussed in public.

6) THIS IS NOT A COMPLETE SOLUTION

 It is obvious that this strategy will not catch every violator; no
 single strategy can do so while respecting user privacy/confidentiality.
 I believe that the procedure outlined in this document is a fair compro-
 mise between our respect for user privacy and our obligations as system
 administrators.

