Message-ID: <2ccopa$83f@senator-bedfellow.MIT.EDU>


Fellow Freedom Fighters,

You all might be interested in an event that took place about two
weeks ago here at MIT.  A 68-page guide called _Dealing With
Harassment at MIT_ was distributed to the MIT community.  Among its
contents was a statement that, ``If your behavior has the effect of
unreasonably offending or intimidating another person, it may be
harassment, even if there was no intent to offend.''  This follows
from MIT's definition of harassment,

  Harassment is any conduct, verbal or physical, on or off campus,
  which has the intent or effect of unreasonably interfering with an
  individual's or group's educational or work performance at MIT or
  which creates an intimidating, hostile, or offensive educational,
  work, or living environment.

The guide also did not insist that the definition of harassment had to
be directed at a person, or part of an unwanted and interfering
pattern of conduct.  So even unintentionally ``offensive'' speech
constitutes harassment and can be actionable under MIT policy.

Upon recieving the guide through the mail, several students read
through it, and decided to burn their guides.  Within an hour, they
had a bonfire going outside their dormitory with 30-40 students
burning their own guides.

This event attracted considerable interest: there was an article in
the _Boston Globe_, at least two TV news reports, several radio news
reports, an Associated Press release, a radio (WRKO; AM 680 Boston
area) debate between one of the burners and one of the guide's
proponents, among others.  Furthermore, there will likely be coverage
in _The Chronicle of Higher Education_, the _Boston Pheonix_, and
local university newspapers.

Much of the coverage the burning recieved was positive, especially
since it brought to the attention of a very large number of people the
fact that universities have come a long way from being bastions of
free speech and have now become actively engaged in censorship.

If similar protests started spreading to campuses across the nation,
imagine what it might do for the free speech movement!  Be warned,
though, that some people (though apparently not as many as one might
expect) are turned off by the burning of any kind of book, even if it
is one's own property and happens to be a speech code.

-Han Y. Huang                                           hhuang@mit.edu
