I think this is the only section that concerns me (I think):

morgan@engr.uky.edu (Wes Morgan) writes:

[...]
>	- The search program should be executed at random; a regular
>	  day/time of execution would allow violators additional op-
>	  portunity to camouflage their activities.
[...]

Contrast with the U. of Illinois (general) privacy policy which
provides for 24-hours notice before suspicionless searches for
policy violators:

============================
"Code of Campus Affairs
[...]
Statement on Individual Rights
[...]
4. Privacy
[...]
B. When the University seeks access to an office assigned or living
quarters leased to an individual to determine compliance with
provisions of applicable multiple dwelling unit laws, ordinances, and
regulations, or for imporvement or repairs, the occupant shall be
notified of such action not less than twenty-four hours in advance.
There may be entry without notice in emergencies where imminent danger
to life, safety, health, or property is reasonably feared and for
custodial service.
[...]
============================

In general I hate the idea that of routine suspicionless searches.  As
searches become routine, the safeguards on them become lax.

At the very least, users should be told after each search, that they
have been searched (even if nothing is found).

I think 24-hour before-the-fact notices would be even betten.  The
problem with after-the-fact notices is that procrastination and an
aversion to negative user reaction will tend to encourage the
searchers to forget to send them out. Before-the-fact have a natural
check.

Wes points out that before-the-fact notice gives policy violators an
additional opportunity to camouflage their activities. This is true,
but it also gives nonviolators an assurance that they are not the
subject of secret searches. As a practical matter, the real purpose of
this policy is to deter; even with a 24-hour before-the-fact notice, I
think it would do this.

- Carl
