kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie) writes:

cmk>At the very least, users should be told after each search, that they
cmk>have been searched (even if nothing is found).

osborne@UNBSJ.CA (Steve Osborne) writes:

so>I disagree that this is necessary.  We usually perform automated search
so>procedures on a routine basis to ensure compliance with our policies.

I don't know if it is legally necessary. I suppose it depend on one's
juristication. In the U.S., our law says:

      The right of the people to be secure in their persons, houses,
      papers, and effects, against unreasonable searches and seizures,
      shall not be violated; and no warrants shall issue, but upon
      probable cause, supported by oath or affirmation, and
      particularly describing the place to be searched and the persons
      or things to be seized.

According to the ACLU Handbook: _The Rights of Students_, 1988.

      "[Question:] Can student be subject to mass searches?
       [Answer:] No. The Supreme Court [has not dealt with this.] But a
       number of federal and state courts have ruled that there must a
       reasonable suspicion directed specifically at each student before a
       school official can search students. ..."

The Canadian Charter of Rights and Freedoms says:

      Everyone has the right to be secure against unreasonable search or
      seizure.
(I know nothing of the Canadian case law on suspicionless student
searches.)

Regardless of the "necessity" of after-the-fact notice, I think it is
the decent thing to do. You mention compliance with policies. How can
users ensure that the authorities comply with search policy, if they
don't even know the nature of the searches? Put another way, the
reason for notice is accountability.

cmk>I think 24-hour before-the-fact notices would be even betten.  The
cmk>problem with after-the-fact notices is that procrastination and an
cmk>aversion to negative user reaction will tend to encourage the
cmk>searchers to forget to send them out. Before-the-fact have a natural
cmk>check.

so>This is ridiculous IMNSHO.  A notice before the fact?  What is the sense
so>in searching if you are going to allow them to hide the evidence?  How 
so>would this deter the real culprits?  I would like to hear your answers to
so>these questions.

It would deter many who would decide that hiding is not worth the effort.

Would it deter all policy violators? No.

But would "No-knock" suspicionless searches deter all policy violators? No.
But would monitoring every keystroke deter all policy violators? No.
But would putting secret surveillance camera in student dorms
          deter all policy violators? No.

I concede that the more obtrusive a search is, the more effective it is. 
I don't concede that effectiveness is the only criterion to consider
when creating a policy for suspicionless searches.

