In <2c9g00$h1b@eff.org> kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie) writes:
>[A newspaper article, posted with the author's permission. -cmk]
 
>===========================================
 
>Independent Commentary 11/11/93
 
>The Harvard Independent -- Commentary Policy:  The opinions
>expressed in the Commentary section are the authors' own and
>do not necessarily reflect the views of the Independent's
>editors.  The Independent welcomes commentaries and responses
>from all Harvard students and  faculty members, on a range of
>national and campus issues.
 
>"Harvard sponsors cyberporn"
 
>The Internet plays host to violent sex stories, and you pay
>the bill.
 
>by David Sherwood
 
[Etc]
 
My letter to the editor in response to this piece was published in today's
Independent. It was edited a bit more than I would have liked, but I did have
the opportunity to review the changes and I think it still hits most of the
major points. Responses on the net or by e-mail are welcome.

To the Editor:

	I am writing in response to David Sherwood's commentary, 
"Harvard sponsors cyber-porn," (Independent, Nov. 11). In my four years 
at Harvard, I have devoted much of my energies to civil-liberties causes; I 
have even served as the Director of the Civil Liberties Union of Harvard. 
but I can say that in all that time, I have never seen an article as ignorant 
and dangerous as Sherwood's.

	The article is difficult to criticize on philosophical grounds simply 
because it contains so many factual errors. For example, Sherwood writes 
that "If we can censor what's published in magazines, surely we can do the 
same for electronic publications." Try as I might, I do not understand what 
he is talking about. Censorship of magazines in this country has been 
extremely rare. Such attempts have inevitably been challenged in court, 
usually successfully. 

	Sherwood continues, "if showing violent sexual perversions to 13-
year old boys isn't shouting 'fire' in a crowded theater, then I don't know 
what is." Mr. Sherwood is apparently thinking of the "clear and present 
danger" standard first elucidated in Schenck v. United States. Jurisprudence 
is no longer so simplistic, if it ever was. It's obvious that permitting 
minors access to pornography does not meet the standards of the "clear and 
present danger" test - the harm is neither immediate nor unavoidable.

	Sherwood also writes that "Harvard can pull the plug on any 
newsgroup it chooses without losing access to the network's other 
benefits." This is wrong. If Harvard were to interfere with student 
transmissions -- however selectively -- it would be considered a 
"publisher," and it might be held liable under the law for the content of all 
transmissions.

	Harvard would not even save any money by censorship. The 
University pays for a connection to other computer systems, not a charge 
per newsgroup. Further, other sites that depend on Harvard for access to 
the Internet should have the right -- and the responsibility -- to decide what 
information they carry. It is not Harvard's role to act as a moral censor.

	 Sherwood writes that Internet groups depict "outlawed forms of 
sexual deviance." Apparently Sherwood disapproves of gays and lesbians, 
bondage and other sexual activities. But sex is part of life. Sherwood cites 
extreme examples, but many posts on the Internet address real issues of 
relationships between adults. Mr. Sherwood, if you are going to call for 
censorship, at least be honest. Do not claim to be a liberal with my best 
interests in mind -- admit that you do not like my freedom to select my 
own behavior.

	I do not even understand his final point, that Harvard may be a 
"blameworthy" institution responsible for horrid sex crimes. Sherwood 
apparently assumes that pornography is directly responsible for sex 
crimes. Suffice it to say that such a connection has never been proven. And 
the idea that speech should be prohibited because it has a nefarious effect is 
contrary to the spirit of the First Amendment. There is no need to protect 
speech that is  liked and that has no effect. The true test is whether we are 
willing to tolerate speech that revolts us.

	In closing, then, I would like to congratulate the Independent for 
publishing what it knew would be a controversial piece. But I would also 
like to chastise the Independent for failing to check so many of its facts. If 
Sherwood's article was not so full of errors and distortions, it could 
perhaps have inspired a thought-provoking debate.

						Sincerely,

						Jol Silversmith '94

