In article <osborne.69.0@unbsj.ca>, Steve Osborne <osborne@UNBSJ.CA> wrote:
>In article <2ceovb$r39@s.ms.uky.edu> morgan@engr.uky.edu (Wes Morgan) writes:
>
>>2) BE PREPARED TO MAKE EXCEPTIONS
>>
>
>I can't say that I agree with this.  If their faculty/staff supervised
>studies/research required them to use the prohibited package then I think
>the aforementioned list off prohibited software would also state that 
>legitamite use of such software may only be performed if permission has
>been given by the security personnnel.  

That's the end result of this whole procedure.  Ideally, the faculty
member would let me know, ahead of time, that they would be using the
otherwise-prohibited program XXX in their course.  If necessary, they
(or I) could work up an emasculated version of the program that would
fulfill their needs *without* undue security risk.  For example, one
could take the Kuang expert system from COPS and make it a standalone
package, to be used with bogus input data.  That would meet the aca-
demic need *and* conform to a (hypothetical) prohibition against COPS.

One may also provide *known* copies of controversial/prohibited code,
modifying it such that it can ONLY access the inert, bogus input data
files you have created.  I did this for one group that used nnstat.
They could execute it and observe its actions, but they could only run
it on the inert ethernet-packets file I created.

>Beyond the above is letting everyone use an excuse that they are just
>doing individualized  studies/research and experimenting.  Everything
>is educational!  Even cracking password files.

I agree completely; as long as they aren't cracking *live* password
files, what's the problem?

Some of you may ask, "How will you know that they're only working on
the bogus data you give them?"  Well, that's where other security mea-
sures come in.  There are several Unix facilities (including /etc/fuser,
which is standard on many Unix systems) that allow the superuser to 
determine which files are being used by a particular process.  Use of
this facility would help ensure that the user is only working on the
"inert" files you've provided.  Other facilities, such as Gene Spaf-
ford's Tripwire, can help ensure that they don't substitute live data
in the inert file you give them, through its checksumming facilities.

Yes, this is a rather involved proposition; however, this seems to
be the only real way to support individualized education/experimentation 
*and* honor my security-related obligations.

>>3) SEARCHING FOR VIOLATIONS OF THE PROHIBITION
>> To minimize the intrusion, it is essential that the examination be
>> unattended; the administrator should not be examining the content of
>> individual files visually.
>
>Actually, visual inspection is impractical in most cases.  An automated
>unattended operation is usually necessary for all practical searches.

This is true for the "first cut," but a visual examination would be
necessary to eliminate any "false positive" results.  As one email
correspondent pointed out, my original posting included an extract
from Crack-4.1; if I had used those signature strings in a search,
their mailbox (or archival copy of my posting) would have triggered
a positive result.  At that point, only a visual examination (prefer-
ably in the user's presence and with their permission) can exonerate
the file in question.

>>	- The search program should be available for execution by all
>>	  users; while this may allow violators to test their conceal-
>>	  ment of the software, it will reassure those users who have
>>	  no desire to violate site policy.
>
>I see no reason why the above is necessary.  Those who do not want to
>violate site policy have the list of prohibited software to go by.
>Allowing the users to search for the software themselves just allows
>violators to conceal their activities as you have said.

Given the large number of possible signature strings, it would seem
difficult to camouflage successfully.  Consider that just the shell
scripts in Crack 4.1 cover 21+ files and are over 33K in size.  Con-
sider the number of possible 10-byte signature strings and the fact
that the user would not know the number of signature strings being
sought.  I doubt that a user could camouflage their files effectively
in such an environment.  

Remember that the users would be able to *execute* the program, but
they would *not* have access to the source; therefore, they would have
to guess at the content of the signature strings.

>>4) DEALING WITH THE RESULTS
>>
>> prohibited software package.  With (and only with) the user's explicit 
>> permission, the administrator may visually inspect the suspect files at 
>> this time.
>
>Visual inspection of files at this institution occurs after the files have
>been flagged by the automated search process.  There is no need to call in
>every user and ask for their permission to search their files.  As long as
>the approriate authority has given permission to check suspected files then
>I see no problem with this.  Do peace officers require permission to search
>someone's house once they have permission from a higher authority to do so?
>(ie a search warrant)

I had envisioned this process as informational, rather than judicial.  I 
have no desire to enter the judicial aspects of University discipline. I
realize that some users may not grant me permission to examine their files;
in those cases, I would have to secure permission from higher authority.
I prefer, however, to give them the "right of first refusal;" it seems
to build trust between us.

>I think all of the above is going overboard.  Allowing the user to refuse
>inspection and having to inform them that you will be making a backup is
>unnecessary IMNSHO.

Given the murky nature of computer rights and responsibilities, I prefer
to cover every base.  I have no idea about the legality of a "directed
backup," but I wouldn't Xerox the contents of a file cabinet without the
user's foreknowledge and/or permission.  It's just common sense to keep
them informed of what's going on; I have no desire to be accused of de-
ception or misinformation.  

>>5) THE ADMINISTRATOR'S WORK IS DONE
>>
>> At this point, the resolution of this matter lies solely with the
>> appropriate judicial/disciplinary authority.
>
>This may be true at some institutions but not at mine.  The disciplinary
>authority does not deal with violations of our computing policies.  They
>would probably consider it a waste of their time and do not take computing
>security issues seriously unless it affects the administrative functions of
>the institution.

If you are the "appropriate authority," that's fine, as long as everyone
involved is aware of that fact.  I would hope that your policy doesn't
make you judge, jury and executioner alone; some outside input seems 
preferably, as a check and balance mechanism.

--Wes

