In article <2cgpf6$sdp@eff.org>, Carl M. Kadie <kadie@eff.org> wrote:
>
>Not necessarily, they may hide from your no-knock searches, too. Even
>trivial encryption could defeat no-knock searches. 

If I wanted to get *really* nasty about this, I would:

	- Monitor the process table 
	- Eliminate processes running system-owned programs,
	  such as passwd, finger, who, et cetera
	- Check that no user process is accessing a "sensitive"
	  file, e.g. /etc/passwd, /etc/shadow, or whatever...
	  (/etc/fuser can do this)
	- Use tools like lsof to determine files being accessed by 
	  user processes
	- Run the previously discussed "signature string" search
	  against the list generated by /etc/fuser and lsof.

This would nail anyone who kept executables (or data files) encrypted
during the routine file search we are discussing.  The only thing that 
would effectively protect against this technique would be to actually 
decrypt the data within the program.  Of course, I can always:

	- Use lsof to obtain list of user memory segments
	- Extract ASCII strings directly from memory
	- Apply "signature string" search to retrived strings (or, for
	  that matter, to the actual binary data)

If we wanted to be *really* serious about it, it could be done.  I have
no desire to go that far, but it is certainly possible.

--Wes

