In article <q2ZTt*B70@world.std.com> cosell@world.std.com writes:
>} 
>} I don't know if it is legally necessary. I suppose it depend on one's
>} juristication. In the U.S., our law says:
>} 
>}       The right of the people to be secure in their persons, houses,
>}       papers, and effects, against unreasonable searches and seizures,
>}       shall not be violated; and no warrants shall issue, but upon
>}       probable cause, supported by oath or affirmation, and
>}       particularly describing the place to be searched and the persons
>}       or things to be seized.
>
>Silly me, and here I thought that that was only a restriction on
>the *government*, not on private citizens over how they administer
>their own property.  "seizure" is especially amusing in this context:
>I can just see going to a judge to get a court order for permission
>to seize *your*own* disk drive.

When the university is a public institution, constitutional protections
apply.


>Things are a bit different for students using universtiy systems,
>but the inquiry seemed only to ask about "users".  And within
>this context, it is clear that "computer files" on *someone*else's*
>system, in an environment where the user/student is *told* ahead
>of time that any file on the system is subject to search at the whim
>and discretion of the sysadmin, actually constitutes a "search"?

I don't know of any precedent regarding the Fourth Amendment and
computer files on a public university's machine.  Without such precedent,
it is hard to make any strong claims about the status of those computer files.


>Searching the student's -person-, or their personal effects in their
>dorm room, or even the files on a *personal* computer of theirs
>might well run afoul of the law, but when a student knowingly
>puts something in an area where they are *told* ahead of time
>"Don't keep private stuff _here_", wouldn't that blunt any
>claim of an unreasonable search?

Not in and of itself.  If the university allows its users to exchange
personal email, for example, then there is an implicit agreement that
the users may retain private material on the system.  So the explicit
disclaimer does not say much.

In any case, if the users have a *right* to be secure from unreasonable
searches, then generally, the government (the public university) cannot
"contract away" that right via disclaimers.

By your reasoning, the institution could also contract away your privacy
rights within a *dorm room* (to use your example).  All they would need
to do would be to include a disclaimer saying, "Don't keep private stuff
_here_."


