he or she saw might be reported to the police.  If you weren't 
aware of this, don't feel left out.  Most people who did know 
did not find out from Housing.  Most residents found out 
Wednesday night when we personally warned over 400 students 
with a voice-mail message.

Among the rights afforded us by the U.S. Constitution, it
is stated (4th ammedment) that ``the right of the people to be
secure ... against unreasonable searches and seizures'' is
guaranteed.  Civil Rights are not waivable -- you cannot just 
give them up.  Any agreement which tries to limit these rights is
void by law, but ... how many entering freshmen do you suppose
know that The Residence Hall Contract which they sign as
a condition of attendance is, legally speaking, unconstitutional?

This contract states that Housing may search student rooms as long
as it provides warning 48 hours in advance.  Housing's ``warning''
consisted of one small poster, placed someplace on each dorm floor.
Why weren't students told personally, or at least by letter or 
voice-mail? (It has occurred to us that this just might be because 
borrowed lounge furniture nets the University a nice sum in student 
fees -- $25/day/piece)  Housing has shown through this incident 
that they are not only willing to abridge the rights of their 
tenants, but also to bend the spirit of their own written policy.

This concerns us.  Housing's violation of these rights is probably 
more a matter of rudeness and lack of respect than intention, but 
when young men and women are the victims, the damage is double.  
Here is why:

Free living is a learned behavior.  It is as much a cultural thing
when we say this is a ``free country'' as it is a legal ideal.  
Americans are free because they are willing to fight to preserve
freedom.  By whittling away the rights of our younger generation
here, the University is essentially teaching them to be complacent,
docile ... sheep.  If they learn that violations like this are 
okay, they will not be able to participate effectively in the 
ongoing democratic process that _keeps_ America free.

Parents complain about their teens acting like children.  

Treating them as adults might help change this.


